  "query_preview": "..\n\nLongwave ridging over the central Plains will strengthen and expand \ninto the Great Lakes through Saturday in response to a closed low \nnear the Four corners opening up across the Rockies with and \ninteraction with additional upper level disturbances/troughing that \nbuilds in across the Pacific Northwest. This will reinforce high \npressure across the Great Lakes resulting in persistence forecast to \nend the week. Highs will again push into the upper 60s today, \npossibly peaking around 70 under mostly sunny skies. Clear skies and \nlight winds overnight will likely lead to patchy or areas of fog,\npossibly dense, given the supplementation of shallow low-level \nmoisture under the light northeast flow. Fog likely expands from the\nThumb and Tri- Cities before pushing into the interior of SE MI \nbefore lifting by daybreak. Temperatures then return back into the \nmid 60s for a high under sunny skies. Outside of radiational fog \nconcerns in the mornings, above normal temperatures under mostly \nclear skies persists into the weekend. \n\nThe upper level ridge will flatten Monday into Tuesday over the \nGreat Lakes with a cold front pushing over the state. Limited \nmoisture under a weaker temperature gradient will not bring any \nconcrete rain chances thus PoP values will remain sub 15%, however, \nflattening of the upper-level will direct better moisture and cloud \ncoverage downstream. Temperatures moderate back towards seasonal \nnormals through at least the early week period. ML-models are much \nmore progressive with the forecasted Pacific trough by the middle of\nnext week relative to the deterministic counterparts, which would \nagain bring about above normal temperatures by the middle of next \nweek if they verify, as the warmer air across the Plains streams \ninto the Great Lakes.",
